        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph",
        "problem": "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\nI. $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n\nII. $x\\in [-2,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,3]$",
        "worked_solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
